#   .adv_post.json  — adversarial-post CLI scratch artefact
.aria-token
.adv_post.json

# R-F832 (2026-05-23) — audit W25: scratch corpus tarballs generated
# during the RunPod ARIA-LLM fine-tune workflow. ~6MB combined. Local
# only — should never be committed (would bloat the repo + contain
# operator chat content). Per scripts/train/RUNPOD.md the operator
# regenerates these on demand by tar-ing /data/aria_training from
# aria-intel.
corpus.tar.gz
corpus_v2.tar.gz
corpus.b64
corpus_clean.tar.gz

# Operator's local SSH key for RunPod pod access — generated on
# demand by the autonomous pod-launch flow, never source-controlled.
runpod_aria
runpod_aria.pub

# R-F970 (2026-05-28) — session-scratch monitoring/report artefacts written
# during live log-watch sessions (leading-underscore convention at repo root).
# Operator-local, regenerated each session; never source-controlled. (The
# matching *.log monitor stream is already covered by the Logs section above.)
/_monitor_*.md
/_monitoring_*.md
/_full_pytest_*.txt
/_inspect_*.py

# R-F970 — runtime-learned regression signatures. constitutional_validator.py
# writes these to /data on fly; on a local checkout (no /data mount) it falls
# back to data/learned_attack_signatures.json. Runtime state, not source.
data/learned_attack_signatures.json

# R-F2920 — file-integrity audit ledger. Machine-local runtime evidence about
# THIS checkout: what went missing, when, and what was restored. Per-machine
# state, not shared truth — committing it would mix one developer's antivirus
# incidents into everyone else's history.
data/file_integrity_ledger.json

# R-F988 — pip editable-install artifact for the aria CLI (not source)
*.egg-info/

# R-F1478: runtime state + session scratch — never source. These were getting
# swept into git by ci_deploy's catch-all `git add -A` (agent DBs can hold PII;
# the _*.md files are Claude<->ARIA session comms). Ignore so no deploy commits them.
data/*.db
data/*.db-shm
data/*.db-wal
data/_*.md

# R-F990 — Claude<->ARIA mailbox (runtime messages, not source)
.agent_bridge/

# stray/scratch from ARIA sessions
/exit

# PID-like strays at repo ROOT from a mistyped shell redirect. Found 2026-08-12:
# a file literally named `72540` containing `113852` — two PIDs, one as the
# filename, from a redirect that was meant to be an argument. No script in the
# tree produces it, so there is nothing to fix upstream; the hazard is that
# `git add -A` (which ci_deploy has historically used — see R-F1478 above) would
# commit it. Same class as `/exit` above.
#
# Digit-runs are enumerated rather than globbed as `/[0-9]*` so this can only
# ever match an ALL-NUMERIC name. `/[0-9]*` would also swallow legitimate files
# like `2026_plan.md`, and an ignore rule that hides real work is worse than the
# stray it prevents.
/[0-9]
/[0-9][0-9]
/[0-9][0-9][0-9]
/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]
/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]
/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]
/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]
/_*.py

# R-F1808 IAST run artifacts
scripts/iast/_*.jsonl
scripts/iast/_*.log

# R-F1902 — comprehensive runtime/debug junk guard. ARIA's commit 31eaa8e2
# (R-F1896..1900) blanket-`git add -A`'d ~61 junk files into origin/main —
# _eu_sample.xml (172k lines), a Windows .lnk, _gh_logs.zip, _bridge_reply.txt,
# .aria/ runtime metrics, scripts/_*.out, data/training/dataset_*.json, etc.
# (the recurring problem R-F1478 logged a Gap for). These patterns stop the next
# blanket-add from re-sweeping them. Root-anchored where possible so legit
# package files (e.g. aria_service/intel/_snapshot_throttle.py) are unaffected.
# NOT ignored (meaningful, left to operator/ARIA): data/aria_training/coder_verifiable_gold.jsonl,
# public/endpoint-manifest.json.
# (.gitignore has NO inline comments — patterns only, one per line.)
# all root-level _scratch (xml/json/txt/zip/sh/dirs); no legit root file starts with _
/_*
# Windows shortcuts — never source
*.lnk
# runtime agent metrics dir
.aria/
# deploy-pipeline runtime marker
.last_deploy_sha
# debug scan workspaces
_test_scan_*/
# data-dir scratch (_n1l4_port.txt, _v04_pod_id.txt, ...)
data/_*
# training-run scratch datasets
data/training/dataset_*.json
# training scratch
data/training/_*.jsonl
# partial/in-flight training files
data/training/*.partial.jsonl
# eval scratch (_v0_2_verdict.txt, ...)
data/eval_reports/_*
# generated Prospector rerun bundles; source eval reports remain tracked
data/eval_reports/prospector_*/
# partial eval outputs
data/eval_reports/*.partial.jsonl
# eval/test capture dumps (_eval_*.out, _rf*.out, ...)
scripts/_*.out

# R-F3512 — DD evidence recordings contain PERSONAL DATA (officer names, DOBs,
# sanctions results). A gold corpus is committed deliberately after a human decides
# the record may be retained, never swept in by an incidental `git add`.
data/eval/dd_recordings/

# Cure Protocol census — 3.6MB generated artifact; regenerate with scripts/cure/census.py
docs/cure/census.json

# R-F4297 (C-251) — pytest --basetemp workspaces. Ephemeral per-run fixture
# trees left behind by ad-hoc `pytest --basetemp=...` invocations: 12 at repo
# root and 119 more under data/. They can never be committed, so the pre-commit
# secret scan has no reason to read them, and leaving them in scope meant the
# only way to green the gate was to write machine-local fixture paths into a
# baseline that has to stay reviewable. .scratch/ is the same case at 3.05 GB —
# nine 335 MB LoRA adapters that a credential scanner was reading in full.
.pytest_*/
.scratch/
.scratch_*
data/eval_reports/pytest-*/
data/training/pytest_tmp_*/

# R-F4308 (C-261) — raw coder-session capture. Redacted training signal from the
# operator's own code sessions: their source excerpts and shell output. It stays
# LOCAL by the same rule the server uses for /data/claude_distill — raw capture is
# local, curated corpora are promoted into data/training/ deliberately. Being
# ignored also keeps it out of the secret scanner's scope, which is correct: a
# file that can never be committed needs no pre-commit scan (R-F4297).
data/coder_sessions/
